/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410511 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410511 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51c80586-803f-589a-a591-22c79fbdc1d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410511 #edb/ref 1074 "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=3)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=y\\,|\\,X=3)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=y)}{P(X=3)}=\\frac{f(3,y)}{{f}_{X}(3)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.55$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $y\\in {1,2,3},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=1\\,|\\,X=3) & =\\frac{f(3,1)}{{f}_{X}(3)}=\\frac{0.4}{0.6}=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3) & =\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{X}(3)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=3) & =\\frac{f(3,3)}{{f}_{X}(3)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410512 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410512 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410512 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{Y|X}(0\\,|\\,1)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70462da6-df40-572c-b721-518f7d0fb229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410512 #edb/ref 1074 "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(0\\,|\\,1)=P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=1).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=0\\,|\\,X=1)=\\frac{P(X=1,Y=0)}{P(X=1)}=\\frac{f(1,0)}{{f}_{X}(1)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(1) & =f(1,0)+f(1,1)+f(1,2) \\\\  & =0.25+0.1+0.25 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(1,0)=0.25.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{X}(1)=0.6$ and $f(1,0)=0.25$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(0\\,|\\,1) & =P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=1) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1,0)}{{f}_{X}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.25}{0.6} \\\\  & ≈0.42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410513 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410513 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f027a74-2cf2-5941-ad79-db682e13a988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410513 #edb/ref 1074 "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=4)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=x\\,|\\,Y=4)=\\frac{P(X=x,Y=4)}{P(Y=4)}=\\frac{f(x,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $x\\in {1,2,3},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(1,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(2,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0.05}{0.2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ P(X=3\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(3,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0.05}{0.2}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410514 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410514 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410514 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410514 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.25$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=9$ | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCompute $P(0<X\\le 6\\,|\\,Y=3)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410514 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b172bfa5-07a0-5df8-8d2d-99d558ae1db4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410514 #edb/ref 1074 "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 6\\,|\\,Y=3) & =\\frac{P((0<X\\le 6),Y=3)}{P(Y=3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(X=3,Y=3)+P(X=6,Y=3)}{P(Y=3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3,3)+f(6,3)}{{f}_{Y}(3)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(3) & =f(0,3)+f(3,3)+f(6,3)+f(9,3) \\\\  & =0+0.1+0+0.1 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(3,3)=0.1$ and $f(6,3)=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting our values into our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 6\\,|\\,Y=3) & =\\frac{f(3,3)+f(6,3)}{{f}_{Y}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1+0}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1}{0.2} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410515 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410515 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410515 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410515 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{32}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{32}$ | $\\frac{3}{32}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{5}{32}$ | $\\frac{5}{32}$ | $\\frac{3}{32}$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,3)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410515 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1e5d993-4954-596b-9a63-c575f6c7babb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410515 #edb/ref 1074 "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,3)=P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=3).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=3\\,|\\,X=3)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=3)}{P(X=3)}=\\frac{f(3,3)}{{f}_{X}(3)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(3) & =f(3,1)+f(3,2)+f(3,3) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{32}+\\frac{5}{32}+\\frac{3}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(3,3)=\\frac{3}{32}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{X}(3)=\\frac{13}{32}$ and $f(3,3)=\\frac{3}{32}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,3) & =P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=3) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3,3)}{{f}_{X}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{32})}{(\\frac{13}{32})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410516 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186410517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410516 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21b7e9f5-9b6f-59bb-8de5-73acd8